# ===========================================================================
# HOW A PIN REACHES THE IMAGE  (T-016 / OD-1 — this used to be wrong)
# ===========================================================================
# scripts/lib/toolbox.sh reads EVERY line above. For each one it passes
# --build-arg <KEY>_VERSION=<value> (KEY upper-cased, non-alphanumerics folded
# to '_', with the single alias nodejs -> NODE), and it puts a fingerprint of
# the whole set into the image tag:
#
#   kinvara-toolbox:node<n>-pnpm<p>-tf<t>[-pw<w>]-x<12 hex>
#
# Reproduce the fingerprint by hand, exactly:
#
#   awk '!/^[[:space:]]*#/ && NF >= 2 { print $1, $2 }' app/.tool-versions \
#     | LC_ALL=C sort | sha256sum | cut -c1-12
#
# So CHANGING OR ADDING ANY LINE HERE CHANGES THE TAG AND FORCES A REBUILD.
# You cannot keep an old toolchain by accident, and you cannot run a stale
# binary while believing this file. Comments and line order deliberately do
# not affect it: re-wording this paragraph must not invalidate a 1 GB image.
#
# BEFORE T-016 THIS WAS NOT TRUE. The tag encoded only nodejs/pnpm/terraform
# and only those four were passed as build args, so bumping gitleaks or trivy
# reused the cached image in silence (decisions.md OD-1). `gate:toolbox` was
# the only thing catching it, after the fact.
#
# WHEN YOU ADD A LINE: add the matching `ARG <KEY>_VERSION` to
# docker/toolbox.Dockerfile and use it. If you forget, BuildKit warns about an
# unconsumed build-arg on a rebuild that now definitely happens — loud, and
# not silence.
